Chapter: SumFieldEq

 Sum equality on arm64 compared two heap POINTERS and answered a plausible
 wrong bit (plugs-backlog 1.90). This arm is built to fail in BOTH directions,
 because the three broken shapes each pass an arm that only tests one.

 A pointer compare answers `ne` for every freshly built pair, so it passes any
 arm made of UNEQUAL values. A tag-only compare answers `eq` for two values of
 the same constructor whatever their payloads, so it passes any arm made of
 EQUAL values. And a raw-word field compare answers `ne` for two equal Texts at
 different addresses. So the equal rows must hold values built at different
 addresses, which is why one side of the Text pair is CONCATENATED rather than
 written as the same literal: two identical literals may be pooled to one
 address, and a pooled pair passes a pointer compare by accident.

 The last two Quad rows differ only in the FOURTH field, past the two that the
 old emitter compared, so they are red on the field cap alone.

Section: Definitions

  Held =
    | Empty
    | Hold (Text)

  Quad =
    | Quad (Integer) (Integer) (Integer) (Integer)

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line (if Hold ("h" & "i") == Hold "hi" then "eq" else "ne")
    print-line (if Hold "hi" == Hold "no" then "eq" else "ne")
    print-line (if Empty == Empty then "eq" else "ne")
    print-line (if Empty == Hold "hi" then "eq" else "ne")
    print-line (if Quad 1 2 3 4 == Quad 1 2 3 4 then "eq" else "ne")
    print-line (if Quad 9 2 3 4 == Quad 1 2 3 4 then "eq" else "ne")
    print-line (if Quad 1 9 3 4 == Quad 1 2 3 4 then "eq" else "ne")
    print-line (if Quad 1 2 9 4 == Quad 1 2 3 4 then "eq" else "ne")
    print-line (if Quad 1 2 3 9 == Quad 1 2 3 4 then "eq" else "ne")
  end
